You will compare one known error in a data agent's work against a ranked list of items produced by a reviewer. Your job is to decide whether any item in the list identifies this known error, and if so, which one.

An item identifies the known error only if both of these hold:
- It is about the same thing: the same table, and the same column, computation, filter, or join.
- It points to the same problem: what the item says is wrong, assumed, or missing is the same as what went wrong in the known error. It does not matter whether the item is phrased as an assumption the agent made ("the agent assumes ...") or as a problem in the work ("this does not match ..."); judge by meaning.

An item does not count if it only says that something may be wrong without saying what, such as "this column may be computed incorrectly". An item about the same column but a different problem does not count.

If more than one item identifies the error, report the highest-ranked one. If you are unsure, say so rather than guessing.

Return a JSON object with:
- "verdict": "match", "no_match", or "unsure".
- "rank": the position of the matching item (1 is the top), or null.
- "quote": the exact words from the matching item that show it identifies the error, or null.
- "reason": one short sentence.

=== Known error ===
Table: google_ads__executive_dashboard
What the agent's work assumed: 扩张机会标志在条件判断不出时留空就行
What was actually required or true: 判断不出时默认 false

=== Reviewer's ranked list for this table ===
[
  {
    "rank": 1,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The dashboard preserves multiple account rows per date rather than achieving its required one-row-per-account-day grain.",
      "about": "data",
      "evidence_in_work": "executive_dashboard 10000; account_report 9674; SELECT ... FROM ap",
      "source": "exactly one row per account_id per date_day post-filter",
      "reason": "Executive totals and trend windows operate on segmented account observations rather than unique daily account results."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 2,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Week-over-week changes use the seventh prior row instead of establishing the observation from seven calendar days earlier.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "lag(total_spend,7) over(partition by account_id order by date_day)",
      "source": "percent change ... vs the value 7 days prior",
      "reason": "Sparse dates and repeated account dates can make the stated weekly trends compare unrelated intervals."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 3,
    "item": {
      "problem": "trending_alert and scale_opportunity can be NULL instead of false when lag values or keyword denominators are missing.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "(spend_wow_growth_pct>50 OR conversions_wow_growth_pct< -30) trending_alert,(star_pct>15) scale_opportunity",
      "source": "trending_alert and scale_opportunity: else false; tests: not_null",
      "reason": "Early account observations and dates without keyword activity violate the required boolean output contract."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 4,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The rounded rolling averages are added as duplicate aliases and the final projection may select the original unrounded columns.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "SELECT *,round(spend_30d_avg,2) spend_30d_avg,round(roas_30d_avg,4) roas_30d_avg,round(performance_score_30d_avg,1) performance_score_30d_avg",
      "source": "spend_30d_avg ... rounded ... 2 decimals; roas_30d_avg ... 4 decimals; performance_score_30d_avg ... 1 decimal",
      "reason": "Duplicate-name handling can leave the contract-named output fields unrounded."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 5,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Portfolio ratios are rounded before threshold comparisons rather than compared using the specified safe divisions.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "round(100.0*star_performer_keywords/nullif(active_keywords,0),2) star_pct ... WHEN star_pct>20",
      "source": "star_performer_keywords/active_keywords > 0.2 ... using safe divide",
      "reason": "Ratios just above a decision boundary can round down and select the wrong recommendation or alert."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 6,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Daily monetary and fractional outputs retain DECIMAL types instead of the specified DOUBLE types.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "ap.spend total_spend ... ap.conversions total_conversions ... ap.conversions_value total_conversions_value",
      "source": "total_spend, total_conversions, total_conversions_value: data_type: DOUBLE",
      "reason": "The dashboard schema inherits an upstream type mismatch."
    }
  }
]
